+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`createTaskList` keeps tasks in a `Map` keyed by title; every method resolves a title through one internal `find`. Project rules forbid clocks and randomness in `src/domain/`, and domain code returns `Result`s instead of throwing.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Deterministic unique ids, stable over the task's life.
+- One resolution path for id-or-title.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Persistence of ids across lists, or ids unique between lists.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Ids from a per-list counter**: `task-1`, `task-2`, ... Randomness (UUIDs) is forbidden in the domain. The counter only increases, so removed ids are never reused. `createTask` takes the id as a parameter, so `task.ts` stays pure.
+- **Keep the title-keyed map, add an id index** (`Map<id, title>` kept in step on rename/remove), over re-keying by id, which would make title uniqueness checks linear.
+- **Id wins over title** when text matches both: the id is the stable reference, so it must never be shadowed by someone choosing a title like `task-1`. Alternative (title first) lets a rename silently redirect an id-based call.
+- `get(id)` looks up by id only; `find` accepts both.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- A title equal to another task's id is unreachable by title → accepted; the title stays reachable by its own task's id.
+- Counter lives in the list instance → ids are unique per list only.
